invariant: INV-26
file: ghash_vector.h
catches: ghash_equiv_test
reason: the AES=hw reduction puts the high half of the middle term, the
  cross products' sum Karatsuba's form recovers, where its low half belongs
  and the reverse, so the unreduced product is wrong in its middle two
  words whenever the cross products are nonzero. It is the mistake a
  256-bit product assembled from three 128-bit sums invites, and it leaves
  right every product whose cross products are zero, such as two operands
  whose low halves are both zero, which is why bin/ghash_equiv_test runs
  every pair of single-bit operands and random pairs beside the fixed ones.
  The edit is shared C after either instruction, so it lands on an ARMv8
  runner and an x86-64 one alike.

--- old
        ghash_xor(ghash_xor(ghash_swap_halves(sums->low), ghash_low_half_only(middle)),
                  ghash_multiply_lane0(sums->low, constant));
    ghash_vector upper = ghash_xor(sums->high, ghash_high_half_down(middle));
--- new
        ghash_xor(ghash_xor(ghash_swap_halves(sums->low), ghash_high_half_down(middle)),
                  ghash_multiply_lane0(sums->low, constant));
    ghash_vector upper = ghash_xor(sums->high, ghash_low_half_only(middle));
